        <h1 class="article-title">Building a lightweight system telemetry monitor</h1>
        <div class="article-date">September 15, 2026</div>
    </div>

    <div class="article-body">
        <p>I wanted a straightforward way to inspect raw OS metrics without deploying heavy monitoring frameworks or background system daemons. The goal was simple: write a backend loop in Python to capture system stats and stream them directly over a WebSocket connection.</p>
        
        <p>The core runs on a minimal asynchronous server using Python's native <code>asyncio</code> and the <code>websockets</code> library, pulling hardware details via <code>psutil</code>. Every second, it serializes data structures into JSON packets containing CPU load, memory utilization, disk space, active PIDs, and network throughput counters.</p>

        <p>Below is the live dashboard client running in the browser. If you have the companion Python script active locally on port <code>8768</code>, it binds instantly and streams live metrics from the host machine:</p>

        <!-- WIDGET INTERACTIVO EMBEBIDO -->
        <div class="dashboard-container">
            <div style="font-size: 0.95em; font-weight: bold; color: #79b8ff; margin-bottom: 12px; border-bottom: 1px solid #2a344a; padding-bottom: 6px;">
                SENTINEL // LIVE TELEMETRY CLIENT
            </div>

            <div class="dash-card">
                <div class="metric-row" style="margin:0;">
